request.prompt:
Job S01: claims_enrich.sas. Parameters per run, in order: AsOfDate=2025-12-31.

Code:
  1 | /******************************************************************************
  2 |   Program  : claims_enrich.sas
  3 |   Purpose  : Claim-level payment history for the risk team. One row per claim
  4 |              on the claims register with its payments up to the reporting
  5 |              date: payment and recovery counts, paid to date, the paid split
  6 |              by payment type (gross, and net of recoveries), the first and
  7 |              last payment dates and a recovery marker. Read by the customer
  8 |              risk scoring (risk_scoring.sas).
  9 |   Inputs   : stg.claims, stg.claim_payments
 10 |   Output   : dw.claims_enriched (rebuilt on every run)
 11 |   Params   : as_of - reporting date (SAS date), set by the scheduler
 12 |   Author   : DOS
 13 | 
 14 |   Change log
 15 |     2014-03-10  DOS   Initial version
 16 |     2015-08-24  DOS   Recoveries counted and flagged for the subrogation desk
 17 |                       (CR-0611)
 18 |     2017-01-16  AK    Paid split by payment type; gross and net paid (CR-1042)
 19 |     2018-11-05  AK    Payments dated after the reporting date left out
 20 |                       (INC-1677)
 21 |     2019-09-30  AK    Line of business and status tidied for the MI pack
 22 |                       (CR-1502)
 23 |     2021-06-14  SMcC  Claims with nothing paid yet kept, with has_payment, for
 24 |                       risk_scoring.sas (CR-1873)
 25 | ******************************************************************************/
 26 | 
 27 | /* claims register */
 28 | proc sort data=stg.claims(keep=claim_id claim_number policy_id customer_id lob_code
 29 |                                loss_date reported_date status)
 30 |           out=work.claims;
 31 |   by claim_id;
 32 | run;
 33 | 
 34 | /* payments to the reporting date, claim by claim in the order they were keyed */
 35 | proc sort data=stg.claim_payments(keep=claim_id payment_date amount payment_type _row_seq
 36 |                                   where=(payment_date <= &as_of))
 37 |           out=work.payments;
 38 |   by claim_id _row_seq;
 39 | run;
 40 | 
 41 | /* roll the payments up to one row per claim */
 42 | data work.pay_summary(keep=claim_id n_payments n_recoveries total_paid indemnity_amt
 43 |                            expense_amt recovery_amt first_payment_date last_payment_date
 44 |                            recovery_flag);
 45 |   set work.payments;
 46 |   by claim_id;
 47 |   length ptype $12;
 48 |   retain n_payments n_recoveries total_paid indemnity_amt expense_amt recovery_amt
 49 |          first_payment_date last_payment_date recovery_flag;
 50 |   format first_payment_date last_payment_date date9.;
 51 | 
 52 |   if first.claim_id then do;
 53 |     n_payments    = 0;
 54 |     n_recoveries  = 0;
 55 |     total_paid    = 0;
 56 |     indemnity_amt = 0;
 57 |     expense_amt   = 0;
 58 |     recovery_amt  = 0;
 59 |     recovery_flag = 0;
 60 |     first_payment_date = payment_date;
 61 |   end;
 62 | 
 63 |   n_payments + 1;
 64 |   total_paid + amount;
 65 | 
 66 |   /* paid split by payment type (keyed in mixed case) */
 67 |   ptype = upcase(strip(payment_type));
 68 |   if ptype = 'INDEMNITY' then indemnity_amt = indemnity_amt + amount;
 69 |   else if ptype = 'EXPENSE' then expense_amt = expense_amt + amount;
 70 |   else if ptype = 'RECOVERY' then recovery_amt = recovery_amt + amount;
 71 | 
 72 |   /* recoveries (salvage, third-party) come through as negative amounts */
 73 |   if amount < 0 then do;
 74 |     recovery_flag = 1;
 75 |     n_recoveries + 1;
 76 |   end;
 77 | 
 78 |   last_payment_date = payment_date;
 79 |   if last.claim_id then output;
 80 | run;
 81 | 
 82 | /* enrich the register: every claim is reported, paid or not */
 83 | data dw.claims_enriched(keep=claim_id claim_number policy_id customer_id lob_code
 84 |                              loss_date reported_date status n_payments n_recoveries
 85 |                              total_paid indemnity_amt expense_amt recovery_amt gross_paid
 86 |                              net_paid first_payment_date last_payment_date recovery_flag
 87 |                              has_payment);
 88 |   merge work.claims(in=c) work.pay_summary(in=p);
 89 |   by claim_id;
 90 |   if c;
 91 | 
 92 |   lob_code = upcase(strip(lob_code));
 93 |   status   = upcase(strip(status));
 94 | 
 95 |   has_payment = p;
 96 |   if not p then do;
 97 |     n_payments    = 0;
 98 |     n_recoveries  = 0;
 99 |     total_paid    = 0;
100 |     recovery_flag = 0;
101 |   end;
102 | 
103 |   gross_paid = indemnity_amt + expense_amt;
104 |   net_paid   = sum(indemnity_amt, expense_amt, recovery_amt);
105 | run;
106 | 
107 | proc datasets lib=work nolist;
108 |   delete claims payments pay_summary;
109 | quit;

Input tables:
- stg.claims: claim_id INT NOT NULL, claim_number VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL, policy_id INT NOT NULL, customer_id INT NOT NULL, lob_code VARCHAR(5) NOT NULL, loss_date DATE NOT NULL, reported_date DATE NOT NULL, status VARCHAR(12) NOT NULL, closed_date DATE, cause_code VARCHAR(10), handler_code VARCHAR(8), description NVARCHAR(200)
- stg.claim_payments: payment_id INT NOT NULL, claim_id INT NOT NULL, payment_date DATE NOT NULL, amount DECIMAL(12,2), payment_type VARCHAR(12) NOT NULL, payee_type VARCHAR(12)

Semantic traps to test (one case per distinct trap id):
- SAS-01 (A missing value is smaller than any number) at lines 36-36: In SAS a missing payment_date counts as smaller than any date, so payment_date <= &as_of keeps rows with no date. A Spark filter drops them, so the translation needs an explicit isNull() OR.
- SAS-03 (Dates are numbers of days since 1 January 1960) at lines 36-36: &as_of is a SAS day count (days since 1960) compared with payment_date. In Spark it has to become a date literal, or the column has to be converted, before the comparison.
- SAS-06 (RETAIN and the sum statement, in input order) at lines 48-49: The accumulators and the first/last dates are retained across rows and reset at first.claim_id. The result depends on the row order (claim_id, _row_seq), so Spark needs an ordered window or an aggregate.
- SAS-06 (RETAIN and the sum statement, in input order) at lines 63-63: The sum statement n_payments + 1 is implicitly retained and counts every row, including rows kept with a missing date.
- SAS-02 (SUM() ignores missing values; + propagates them) at lines 64-64: The sum statement treats a missing amount as 0, so total_paid is never missing. In Spark this means a NULL-skipping F.sum or a coalesce.
- SAS-06 (RETAIN and the sum statement, in input order) at lines 64-64: The sum statement total_paid + amount is retained and accumulates in row order within the claim.
- SAS-02 (SUM() ignores missing values; + propagates them) at lines 68-68: indemnity_amt = indemnity_amt + amount uses plain +, so one missing amount makes indemnity_amt missing for the rest of the claim. F.sum would skip the NULL and give a different result.
- SAS-02 (SUM() ignores missing values; + propagates them) at lines 69-69: In expense_amt = expense_amt + amount, + propagates a missing amount for the rest of the claim. F.sum would not reproduce this.
- SAS-02 (SUM() ignores missing values; + propagates them) at lines 70-70: In recovery_amt = recovery_amt + amount, + propagates a missing amount for the rest of the claim. F.sum would not reproduce this.
- SAS-01 (A missing value is smaller than any number) at lines 73-73: In SAS, amount < 0 is true when amount is missing, so a payment with no amount sets recovery_flag and adds to n_recoveries. A Spark when() treats it as false.
- SAS-06 (RETAIN and the sum statement, in input order) at lines 75-75: n_recoveries + 1 is retained and reset per claim. It is part of the carried state that has to become a conditional count.
- SAS-01 (A missing value is smaller than any number) at lines 88-89: Latent: MERGE BY matches a missing claim_id with a missing claim_id, but a Spark equi-join never matches NULL keys. The translation would need a null-safe join if any keys are missing.
- SAS-05 (MERGE BY with IN= flags) at lines 88-90: This MERGE with in= flags and 'if c' is a left join from claims to pay_summary. has_payment = p comes from the in= flag, and claims with no payments keep missing values in the pay_summary columns.
- SAS-02 (SUM() ignores missing values; + propagates them) at lines 103-103: gross_paid uses +, so it is missing for claims with no payments (their indemnity_amt and expense_amt are not reset to 0) and for claims where an amount was missing. The translation must not coalesce.
- SAS-02 (SUM() ignores missing values; + propagates them) at lines 104-104: sum() skips missing arguments and is missing only if all three are missing, so it is missing for claims with no payments. Spark needs coalesce logic that keeps NULL only when every argument is NULL.
